<Application x:Class="Bws.Gui.App"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:ui="http://schemas.lepo.co/wpfui/2022/xaml"
             StartupUri="MainWindow.xaml">

    <!--
        ADR-24. The base of this window's appearance is WPF UI, not the Fluent theme built into
        WPF, and docs/10-WPF-UI.md is required reading before this file changes.

        THERE IS NO ThemeMode HERE AND THERE MUST NOT BE. It is not a leftover. ThemeMode="Dark"
        together with the dictionaries below KILLS THE PROCESS at startup, with
        InvalidOperationException out of ThemeManager.ApplyStyleOnWindow saying
        AllowsTransparency cannot be changed after the window is shown.

        Removing it also fixed something nobody had connected to it: ThemeMode applies a MICA
        backdrop, a window with Mica is translucent to the desktop compositor, and WPF turns
        ClearType OFF on a translucent surface. Measured by counting text pixels carrying
        colour fringing - 0% before, 80.5% after. That was the blurry text.

        Ours are merged LAST on purpose: a dictionary merged later wins, and ours hold the
        values that are ours. Neither of them loads on its own, because both resolve keys that
        arrive with ControlsDictionary.

        THE THEME WAS ONE FILE UNTIL 2026-08-11 AND THE ORDER OF ITS PARTS IS NOT A STYLE
        CHOICE. Every style file reaches the names in Values.xaml through StaticResource,
        which resolves WHILE THE FILE IS BEING READ, so values go before styles and all of
        them go after the library. Backlog 156 for the first split, 163 for the second,
        backlog 166 for the third on 2026-08-12 - the start mark could not be repaired without
        adding lines to a file already standing on the markup ceiling. Anywhere else that
        builds this stack - WpfHost in the tests, tools/wpfui-probe - repeats these lines
        in this order for the same reason.

        Type.xaml IS A VALUE FILE AND GOES BEFORE EVERY STYLE FILE, since 2026-09-01. It holds the
        type scale - how big a letter is, how heavy, in what face - lifted out of Values.xaml when
        that file hit the ceiling. It depends on nothing, and Text.xaml reaches five of its names
        through StaticResource, so it only has to be in before the styles. Backlog 271 named this
        seam the evening before it was needed.

        Spacing.xaml IS THE FIFTH VALUE FILE, since 2026-09-15, and stands right after Values.xaml
        because that is where its entries stood until that day: every Margin and every Pad, lifted
        out when the plan sheet needed three widths and Values.xaml had no line for one. Backlog
        173. It depends on nothing, every style file reaches it through StaticResource, so it too
        only has to be in before the styles.

        Text.xaml IS BEFORE Controls.xaml AND THAT IS THE SAME RULE ONE STEP FURTHER IN, since
        2026-08-25. It holds every TextBlock style, Controls.xaml reaches one of them through
        StaticResource, and a dictionary merged after this one would be a name that does not
        exist yet. Backlog 213 for why the split happened at all.

        Cells.xaml IS LAST OF OURS AND THAT IS A DIRECTION RATHER THAN AN ACCIDENT. An
        implicit style merged later wins, so the most specialised half - what a person
        actually reads in a row - sits on top of the general ones. Nothing in it is implicit
        today, so the order costs nothing now and is the one that will still be right the day
        something is.

        Overview.xaml IS AFTER Text.xaml AND THAT IS ITS ONLY CONSTRAINT, since 2026-09-01. It holds
        the two styles the first-run screen's buttons wear and resolves FocusRing, PadCell
        and SurfaceHover through StaticResource, so the value files have to be in already. Nothing in
        it is an implicit style and nothing outside names a key of it, so its place among the other
        style files carries no further meaning - which is why the sentence below about Cells.xaml
        being last is still true. It exists because the markup ratchet fired on OverviewView.xaml
        three edits running while that screen was being rebuilt as cards.

        Marks.xaml IS BEFORE Cells.xaml AND THAT IS THE SAME RULE AGAIN, since 2026-08-25.
        It holds the coloured shapes that sit beside three of the words in a row, and the
        cell templates in Cells.xaml name all three through StaticResource - so a dictionary
        merged after this one would be three names that do not exist yet. The split happened
        because the markup ratchet fired: Cells.xaml stood exactly on the ceiling and `A11`
        needs a badge beside the name and a template to hold it.
    -->
    <Application.Resources>
        <ResourceDictionary>
            <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
                <ui:ThemesDictionary Theme="Dark" />
                <ui:ControlsDictionary />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Values.xaml" />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Spacing.xaml" />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Type.xaml" />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Colours.xaml" />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Surfaces.xaml" />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Columns.xaml" />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Text.xaml" />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Overview.xaml" />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Chips.xaml" />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Controls.xaml" />

                <!--
                    AFTER Controls.xaml AND NOT BEFORE IT. Every style in there extends the implicit
                    MenuItem style, which this order is what makes reachable - see the file's own
                    opening lines. It left Controls.xaml on 2026-09-05 when the size ratchet fired.
                -->
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Menus.xaml" />

                <!--
                    AFTER Controls.xaml for the reason Menus.xaml gives about MenuItem: the write
                    buttons of the action bar extend the implicit Button style. 2026-09-23, with the
                    shield of UX-GUI-004 (b) - the file's head says why it is a file of its own.
                -->
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Actions.xaml" />

                <!--
                    THE LIST UNDER THE SEARCH BOX, beside the other floating surface. It names
                    FixedText and SubduedText from Text.xaml through StaticResource, so anywhere
                    before that file is two names that do not exist yet. Sixteenth file, 2026-09-15,
                    because Controls.xaml stood forty-eight lines under the ceiling with three
                    templates to add - point 9 of `docs/11` 2.14.
                -->
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Suggestions.xaml" />
                <!-- The search field, after the list under it: its panel of messages wears SuggestionPanel. -->
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/SearchField.xaml" />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Scroll.xaml" />
                <!--
                    PlanLines.xaml immediately before Plan.xaml, which is App.xaml's order and is
                    load bearing in the ordinary way: both reach names declared above them, and one
                    theme file split into two on 2026-09-07 rather than either half being new.
                -->
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/PlanLines.xaml" />
                <!--
                    PlanOffers.xaml between them, 2026-09-24: the lines with a button under them,
                    split off PlanLines.xaml by the markup ratchet. It extends the copy button
                    declared in PlanLines.xaml, so it has to come after it.
                -->
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/PlanOffers.xaml" />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Plan.xaml" />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/List.xaml" />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Marks.xaml" />
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Cells.xaml" />

                <!--
                    AFTER Marks.xaml, whose three mark styles it extends by StaticResource, and
                    before the catalogue for the reason the catalogue gives. The panel about one
                    entry, 2026-09-16 - its head says why it is a file of its own.
                -->
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Details.xaml" />

                <!--
                    LAST, AND THE POSITION IS THE ONLY THING ABOUT IT THAT MATTERS. The component
                    catalogue is drawn out of names declared above it - SubduedText, HeadingText,
                    SurfacePanel, PaddingPanel - and StaticResource resolves while the file is
                    read, so anywhere earlier is a set of names that do not exist yet.

                    IT SHIPS IN EVERY CONFIGURATION even though only a launch argument reaches it.
                    Leaving it out of Release would mean the sheet could only be photographed from
                    a Debug build, and this project has already spent turns on a picture that was
                    a correct picture of a different file.
                -->
                <ResourceDictionary Source="Themes/Catalogue.xaml" />
            </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
        </ResourceDictionary>
    </Application.Resources>
</Application>
